course: dictionary
lesson: 9894
title: Takeout and takeaway
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. Use the words around a new word to find its meaning. «No more takeout.» The word no tells you that a family used to buy takeout and has stopped, so takeout must be something you can buy and eat at home.
explain: Takeout means food cooked and bought at a restaurant or shop to eat somewhere else, usually at home. Pizza or Chinese food bought for a busy night at home is takeout.
explain: The word changes with where you live. In the USA and Canada people say takeout. In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand people say takeaway. Both words mean the same food.
fact: Takeout is a noun and an adjective: you can buy a takeout, or eat takeout food.
fact: Takeout means food cooked and bought to eat somewhere else, usually at home.
fact: A takeout can also mean the shop or restaurant that sells this food.
fact: In curling, a takeout is a shot that knocks an opponent's stone out of play.
fact: Takeout is fine sometimes, but it often has more salt, fat and sugar than food cooked at home.
exercise: «No more takeout.» What is takeout in this sentence? | A game played on a field | A hot drink for the car | A type of shoe | *Food bought ready-cooked to eat at home
exercise: In the UK, what do people usually call takeout? | *Takeaway | Takeoff | Takeover
exercise: What can takeout also mean? | A pair of tongs for cooking | A kind of cake | *The shop or restaurant that sells the food
test: In the UK, what is the word for takeout? | takeoff | takeover | *takeaway
test: Which is a takeout meal? | A pizza eaten at the restaurant table | A pizza grown in a garden | *A pizza bought at a shop to eat at home
exam: What is takeout food? | Food you grow in a garden | *Food cooked and bought to eat somewhere else | Food eaten at a restaurant table | Food with no salt at all
